  "answer": "To reduce the risk of image classifiers being forced to output wrong labels via imperceptible perturbations, adversarial training has been proposed (https://www.alignmentforum.org/posts/NTwA3J99RPkgmp6jh/an-62-are-adversarial-examples-caused-by-real-but). This works through an implementation mechanism of Lp-bounded inner maximization during training (https://www.alignmentforum.org/posts/NTwA3J99RPkgmp6jh/an-62-are-adversarial-examples-caused-by-real-but). Specifically, during each step of fine-tuning, worst-case Lp perturbations are generated—such as with Projected Gradient Descent (PGD)—and used in the loss minimization (https://www.alignmentforum.org/posts/NTwA3J99RPkgmp6jh/an-62-are-adversarial-examples-caused-by-real-but). Jointly optimizing on clean and adversarial examples forces the model to shift its focus away from non-robust features that, while genuinely predictive, are highly sensitive and can be easily flipped by small perturbations (https://www.alignmentforum.org/posts/NTwA3J99RPkgmp6jh/an-62-are-adversarial-examples-caused-by-real-but). By suppressing these non-robust features, the classifier is encouraged to rely more on robust features to make its predictions (https://www.alignmentforum.org/posts/NTwA3J99RPkgmp6jh/an-62-are-adversarial-examples-caused-by-real-but)."
